Setup
Restarted R? Catch up from the first session.


library(fpp3)
library(mixtime) # Load last

# Use mixtime for the time index of each dataset
aus_production &lt;- aus_production |&gt;
  mutate(Quarter = yearquarter(Quarter))
PBS &lt;- PBS |&gt;
  mutate(Month = yearmonth(Month))
vic_elec &lt;- vic_elec |&gt;
  mutate(Time = mixtime(Time))
pedestrian &lt;- pedestrian |&gt;
  mutate(Date_Time = mixtime(Date_Time))
tourism &lt;- tourism |&gt;
  mutate(Quarter = yearquarter(Quarter))

Visualising temporal patterns
Recall the time plot allowed us to identify temporal patterns including trend, seasonality, and cycles.

pbs_scripts &lt;- PBS |&gt;
  summarise(Scripts = sum(Scripts))
pbs_scripts |&gt;
  autoplot(Scripts)

Visualising seasonality


aus_production |&gt;
  model(STL(log(Gas))) |&gt;
  components() |&gt;
  autoplot()














STL decomposition


Recall that STL allows us to separate trend from seasonality, let’s plot only the seasonal component.






aus_production |&gt;
  model(STL(log(Gas))) |&gt;
  components() |&gt;
  gg_season(season_year, period = years(1L))















Plotting seasonality from decomposition


Removing the trend and noise allows us to clearly see the seasonal pattern, and even how it changes over time.












Can we do better?


How can we more clearly see the changes in the seasonal pattern over time?

Visualising seasonality






Facet by season


A ‘seasonal sub-series plot’ shows changing seasonality.




aus_production |&gt;
  model(STL(log(Gas))) |&gt;
  components() |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(x = Quarter, y = season_year)) +
  geom_time_line() +
  facet_grid(
    cols = vars(
      format(Quarter, "Q{cyc(quarter, year)}")
    )
  )

The seasonal subseries plot






Seasonal decomposition advised


Seeing changes in seasonality when the data has trend, noise, and other patterns is tricky! This plot works best in combination with STL decomposition.




aus_production |&gt;
  model(STL(log(Gas))) |&gt;
  components() |&gt;
  gg_subseries(season_year, period = years(1L))

Changing seasonality

pbs_scripts |&gt;
  model(STL(Scripts)) |&gt;
  components() |&gt;
  gg_subseries(season_year, period = years(1L))















The number of scripts in December is dropping rapidly.
They appear to be redistributed into Oct, Nov, and Jan.

Temporal aggregation

Aggregation to a weekly frequency will sum over the daily and weekly patterns.


vic_elec |&gt;
  index_by(Week = yearweek(Time)) |&gt;
  summarise(Demand = sum(Demand)) |&gt;
  autoplot(Demand)















Question


The first and last weeks are incomplete. How did we keep only complete years in annual aggregation?

Temporal aggregation

vic_daily &lt;- vic_elec |&gt;
  index_by(Day = date(Time)) |&gt;
  summarise(Demand = sum(Demand))
vic_weekly &lt;- vic_daily |&gt;
  # Only days in weeks with all 7 days
  filter(time_is_complete_at(Day, cal_isoweek$week)) |&gt;
  index_by(Week = yearweek(Day)) |&gt;
  summarise(Demand = sum(Demand))
vic_weekly |&gt;
  autoplot(Demand)









Days first: applying time_is_complete_at() to half-hours also drops the weeks when daylight saving starts (mixtime bug).

Temporal aggregation
The pedestrian dataset contains the hourly number of pedestrians at various places in Melbourne, Australia.

pedestrian |&gt;
  filter(Sensor == "Southern Cross Station") |&gt;
  autoplot(Count)

Temporal aggregation

pedestrian |&gt;
  filter(Sensor == "Southern Cross Station") |&gt;
  index_by(Day = date(Date_Time)) |&gt;
  summarise(Count = sum(Count)) |&gt;
  filter(time_is_complete_at(Day, cal_isoweek$week)) |&gt;
  index_by(Week = yearweek(Day)) |&gt;
  summarise(Count = sum(Count)) |&gt;
  gg_season(
    Count,
    period = years(1L, calendar = cal_isoweek)
  )

Temporal aggregation
Since there doesn’t appear to be (substantial) annual seasonality, we can use aggregation to look for weekly seasonality.






