Calendar plots
Using calendar layouts for time plots helps to visualise a large number of frequent (sub-daily) observations.
This is especially useful to show calendar specific patterns such as holidays.

vic_elec |&gt;
  filter(
    yearmonth(Time) &lt;= yearmonth("2012 Apr")
  ) |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(
    x = Time, y = Demand,
    colour = Holiday, group = 1
  )) +
  geom_time_line() +
  coord_calendar(
    rows = weeks(1L), cols = months(2L)
  )

Calendar plots


pedestrian |&gt;
  filter(Sensor == "Southern Cross Station") |&gt;
  filter(
    yearmonth(Date_Time) &lt;= yearmonth("2015 Apr")
  ) |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(x = Date_Time, y = Count)) +
  geom_time_line() +
  coord_calendar(
    rows = weeks(1L), cols = months(2L)
  ) +
  scale_y_continuous(breaks = NULL) +
  labs(x = "Time", y = "Pedestrian count")

Seasonal decomposition
The STL model can also decompose multiple seasonalities.

dcmp &lt;- vic_elec |&gt;
  model(STL(
    log(Demand) ~ season(period = "year") +
      season(period = "week") +
      season(period = "day")
  )) |&gt;
  components()
dcmp |&gt;
  autoplot()

Seasonal decomposition






Exercise 14


Use an STL decomposition and gg_season() to clearly show the seasonal patterns in pedestrian traffic.

ped_dcmp &lt;- pedestrian |&gt;
  filter(Sensor == "Southern Cross Station") |&gt;
  fill_gaps(Count = 0) |&gt;
  model(STL(Count)) |&gt;
  components()
ped_dcmp |&gt;
  autoplot()

Seasonal decomposition

ped_dcmp |&gt;
  gg_season(season_year, period = years(1L))

Seasonal decomposition






Interaction effects


Combine interacting seasonalities, separated by colour or facets (e.g. weekdays and weekends).





ped_dcmp |&gt;
  as_tsibble() |&gt;
  mutate(
    Weekday =
      day_of_week(Date_Time) &lt; day_of_week(6L)
  ) |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(
    x = Date_Time, y = season_day + season_week,
    colour = Weekday, group = 1
  )) +
  geom_time_line(alpha = 0.2) +
  coord_loop(time_loops = days(1L)) +
  scale_x_mixtime(
    time_breaks = hours(6L),
    time_labels = "{cyc(hour, day)}h"
  )

